<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Passchendaele</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Third Battle of Ypres · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Passchendaele, or Third Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a>, was a British-led offensive on the Western Front in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> from July to November 1917. Fought in heavy rain over ground churned into mud by shelling, it gained only a few miles before Canadian troops took the ridge and village of Passchendaele, at a cost of hundreds of thousands of casualties on both sides.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-passchendaele.jpg" alt="Battle of Passchendaele" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frank Hurley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chateauwood.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Third Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a> was fought from July to November 1917 for control of the ridges south and east of Ypres in West Flanders, as part of a strategy decided by the Entente in November 1916 and May 1917. Passchendaele lies on the last ridge east of Ypres, 8 kilometres from Roulers, whose station was on the main supply route of the German 4th Army. The campaign was controversial from the start. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">David Lloyd George</a> and Ferdinand Foch opposed it, and Field Marshal Sir Douglas Haig did not receive the War Cabinet's approval until 25 July. The first stage was the Battle of Messines in June, after mines filled with nearly 454 tonnes of explosives were fired under the ridge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first Allied attack was the Battle of Pilckem Ridge on 31 July. General Hubert Gough's offensive was not planned as a breakthrough. The Germans held the Gheluvelt Plateau in August, though their casualties worsened a manpower shortage. On 25 August Haig shifted the main effort to the Second Army, whose commander Plumer planned to take the plateau in four limited steps at six-day intervals. Unusually wet weather in August and the autumn rains in October turned the ground to mud. The campaign ended in November when the Canadian Corps captured Passchendaele, and a British night attack towards Westroosebeke on 1 and 2 December failed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Casualty figures have been published with acrimony. James Edmonds put British casualties at 244,897 and estimated German losses at 400,000, while A. J. P. Taylor, who rejected Edmonds's calculations, gave 300,000 British and 200,000 German. The British claim of 24,065 prisoners is undisputed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">Lloyd George</a> later called Passchendaele one of the worst disasters of the war, while a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">German General Staff</a> publication wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> had been brought near to certain destruction by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> battle. Jack Sheldon argued that it pinned the German army to Flanders and caused unsustainable casualties. The German submarine bases on the coast had not been captured. The Menin Gate Memorial commemorates Commonwealth dead of the Ypres Salient who have no known grave.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Passchendaele” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…World War the United Kingdom mobilised millions of men, at first by volunteering and from 1916 by conscription in Great Britain, though never in Ireland; the battles of the Somme and <b>Passchendaele</b> cost hundreds of thousands of casualties, and more than 700,000 servicemen from the United Kingdom died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>British and French forces opened the <b>Third Battle of Ypres</b> in Flanders.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-31">31 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Canadian forces took the ruined village of Passchendaele, ending the <b>Third Battle of Ypres</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-6">6 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
